{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792706666,"nonce":"Nt04tfEoaW8d4yxUbPqZKo/E0sU=","title":"zynode: a short program waits at exit for an in-flight LLVM compile","message":"With --llvm, a program that finishes while an LLVM tier-up compile is still running does not exit until that compile is done. NUC (x86-64): 120 functions inlined into one hot top-level loop, the program's output appears at about 166 ms, the phases zynode times (ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES=1) all end at about 0.18 s with shutdown at 0.00 ms, yet total wall time is 1.1 to 1.3 s in most runs (median 1.25 s over 5 runs, minimum 0.18 s). The wait is after main returns: process exit runs the C++ exit handlers of the LLVM library, which block on the compile in flight. It is the same mechanism as the old tier-up exit crash, seen here as a delay. The default tier is unaffected, and so are programs whose installs finish first.\n\nFix (zynode only): after the output is flushed, leave with _exit, skipping the exit handlers. This is what Ash does at shutdown (abandon flag, leaked interpreter, _exit), found in a survey of Ash and Rayzor; Rayzor blocks at exit like we did. Embedders using TieredRuntime::shutdown still join their workers; the other CLIs (zypy, zylua, zynml) were not changed and likely have the same delay.","files":null}]}